{
  "description": "`and`, `or`, `mod` and `is` are legal dictionary keys, and inside a dictionary a newline SEPARATES ENTRIES — `parseDictEntry` skips newlines between a key and its `:` — so a worded operator opening a line there begins the next entry rather than continuing the previous entry's value (issue #933). Every separator spelling is covered: `mod: 2`, `and : 3`, `or:4`, `is :two`, the glued `mod:two` (one `variable` token the lexer splits later), and a separator on its own line (`mod` / `:two`). Getting any of these wrong changes the dictionary SILENTLY, because both readings parse cleanly, so each entry is read back through a place expression — a dict's printed form does not show its entries. The last three cases are the opposite direction, and are what make this fixture fail without the fix: with no separator after it the same word IS an operator, so `{ a: 5` / `mod 3 }` is ONE entry worth 2; and the guard is scoped to the dictionary that owns the newline, so inside the nested list of `{ a: [1` / `mod :two] }` and the nested group of `{ a: (1` / `mod :two) }` the word is an operator again, giving the one-element list [1] and the number 1. Those two deliberately use `mod :two` rather than `mod 2`, because only a shape that reads both ways without a diagnostic can detect a guard that leaked into a nested container. `two` is defined twice on purpose — as a procedure, which a dict-key `mod:two` calls, and as a variable, which the operator form `mod :two` reads.",
